// NEGATIVE TEST -- a top-level item and a function may not share a name
// (N3-09), the item-first direction.
//
// A program has ONE namespace for these. Both become C file-scope definitions
// of the same identifier, so the emitted C is
// `static const long long f = 1;` beside `long long f() { … }` and gcc answers
// "redefinition of 'f' as different kind of symbol" -- a diagnostic about
// GENERATED code for a collision visible in three lines of source.
//
// Refused in both orders, because the first pass walks items in source order
// and either declaration can come first: the function-first direction is pinned
// by tests/reject/function_collides_with_const.pd.
//
// WHY THE RULE COVERS TYPES TOO, and not only functions. A struct and an enum
// each emit `typedef struct <Name> { … } <Name>;` (src/codegen/mod.rs:2704 for
// structs, and generate_enum for enums), so the NAME becomes a C typedef at
// file scope -- the same scope the item's `static const long long <Name>`
// lands in. Six directions exist and all six are refused before gcc sees
// anything: item-vs-function and function-vs-item here and in the sibling,
// item-vs-alias in tests/reject/const_collides_with_type_alias.pd,
// item-vs-enum in tests/reject/const_collides_with_enum.pd, and the struct
// direction, which the same `refuse_global_collision` walk covers.
//
// WHAT IS NOT REFUSED, measured, because a namespace rule that over-reaches is
// as wrong as one that under-reaches: a LOCAL `let` may still take a function's
// name (nothing collides -- the local is inside a C function body), and an enum
// variant in a pattern is not a binder at all.
const f: i64 = 1;

fn f() -> i64 {
    return 2;
}

fn main() {
    print_int(f());
}
